  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\nSimilar to Monday night, patchy fog is once again possible\ntonight into Wednesday morning as increased radiational cooling\nallows temperatures to fall into the upper 20s to low 30s with\nsimilar dewpoints expected. Highest confidence in fog potential\nare within the river valleys. In addition, isolated freezing fog\nis possible especially in areas that fall into the 20s. This\ncould make some surfaces slick on Wednesday morning. Motorists\nshould use caution during the morning commute given the\npotential for reduced visibilities and slick conditions. Will\nmonitor observations across the area tonight for any need for a\nDense Fog Advisory or Special Weather Statement. Confidence in\nlocation is too low to pinpoint this afternoon. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2... \nA dominant high pressure system centered over the Mid-Atlantic\nCoast will continue to influence the weather across northern\nOhio and northwest Pennsylvania into the start of this weekend,\nallowing for a period of prolonged dry conditions. Given \npositioning, persistent southwest flow will allow for WAA to \ngradually increase temperatures throughout the week. By Friday \nand Saturday, much of the area will be well into the 60s.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 3... \nAs the aforementioned high pressure finally becomes progressive\nagain and moves off Mid- Atlantic Coast Saturday, an upper level\ntrough associated with a low centered over the northern Great\nLakes will approach the area from the west. This system has\ndecent model agreement that a cold front will move east on\nSunday which would likely become a focal point for rain showers\nto develop. Exact timing is a bit uncertain, but once this\nfrontal boundary shifts east, much cooler air and more\nseasonable temperatures will return to the area for next week.",
